# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnp/
.pnp.js

# Build output.
#
# EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN HERE SAYS ITS DEPTH ON PURPOSE. A bare `dist/`
# matches a directory of that name AT ANY DEPTH, and that shape is how a bare
# `media/` came to hide `packages/domain/src/media/` - two source files, in a
# package gated at 100% coverage, that git did not list at all (see the store
# block below). `**/` is written where the depth match is what we want, so a
# reader sees it instead of inheriting it by accident.
#
# THE RESIDUAL RISK, because a pattern cannot know intent: these four would
# still swallow a SOURCE directory of the same name anywhere in the tree.
# There is none today - the only matches on disk are `apps/web/.next` (with
# its own `build` inside it), `packages/domain/dist` and
# `packages/tokens/dist`. If a source directory ever needs one of these
# names, anchor the pattern to the path that actually produces artefacts
# (`packages/*/dist`, `apps/web/.next`) rather than adding a negation.
#
# The two file patterns are deliberately depth-free: `*.tsbuildinfo` is
# written next to whichever tsconfig produced it, and `next-env.d.ts` next to
# whichever Next app generated it.
**/.next/
**/out/
**/dist/
**/build/
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Environment and secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Test artefacts.
#
# ANCHORED, because every one of these has exactly one home: `coverage/` is
# where both Vitest configs write, `test-results/` and `playwright-report/`
# are `playwright.config.ts`'s `outputDir` and html reporter, `blob-report/`
# is the blob reporter's, and `lhci-reports/` is `lighthouserc.json`'s
# `outputDir` - all of them resolved from the repository root. A directory of
# any of these names deeper in the tree is source, and git should list it.
/coverage/
/playwright-report/
/test-results/
/blob-report/
*.lcov
/.lighthouseci/
/lhci-reports/

# Local media and database volumes.
#
# SCOPED TO THE STORE'S REAL PATH, not a bare `media/`. A bare directory
# pattern matches a directory of that name AT ANY DEPTH, so `media/` also
# ignored `packages/domain/src/media/` - the magic-byte sniff and the ingest
# policy, source files git then did not list at all, in a package whose
# coverage gate is 100%. Nothing failed: `git status` was clean, `git add`
# said nothing, and the files simply would not have been committed. The one
# media store this repository writes is `MEDIA_DIR` in
# `apps/web/collections/media.ts`, which resolves to `apps/web/media`.
# `uploads/` is scoped for the same reason, before a module of that name
# disappears the same way.
apps/web/media/
apps/web/uploads/
.postgres-data/

# Agent git worktrees.
#
# ANCHORED, and to the container rather than to one worktree: `.claude/`
# itself is SOURCE - `.claude/skills/` holds the two browser-QA skills
# CLAUDE.md §10 makes mandatory, and they are tracked - so only the one
# subdirectory git puts worktrees in is ignored, at exactly one depth, from
# the repository root. A pattern of `.claude/` would hide those skills the
# way a bare `media/` hid `packages/domain/src/media/` (see the store block
# above); a pattern of `**/worktrees/` would hide a source directory of that
# name anywhere.
#
# WHAT IS BEING HIDDEN IS A DUPLICATE, NOT A SOURCE FILE. A git worktree is a
# second checkout of THIS repository: every file under it is tracked in its
# own checkout, on its own branch, and is committed and reviewed there. From
# here it is the same repository twice, and the second copy has no business
# in this one's `git status`, in `prettier --check .`, or in ESLint's walk.
#
# WITHOUT THIS, `npm run verify` CANNOT PASS while a worktree exists.
# `prettier --check .` walks into it and flags its generated
# `apps/web/payload-types.ts` (`.prettierignore`'s entry for that path carries
# a slash, so it is anchored to the root and does not match the nested copy),
# and `apps/web/lib/auth/adminGuardRegistration.test.ts` fails six cases:
# `git ls-files --others` reports a nested repository as one DIRECTORY entry,
# which that suite refuses to read rather than silently skip.
#
# NO SCAN LOSES ANYTHING BY THIS. That suite's coverage case reads git's own
# listing, and a nested repository's files were never in it - only the
# directory entry was. Ignoring the directory removes the entry; the files
# under it were unlisted either way, and they are listed in their own
# checkout, where that suite runs against them on their own commit.
/.claude/worktrees/

# The agent harness's own machine-local settings, which it REWRITES while a
# session runs. It is ignored today only by this developer's global excludes
# file (`~/.config/git/ignore`), which no other checkout has and which Prettier
# does not read at all — Prettier 3 defaults its ignore path to `.gitignore`
# and `.prettierignore` and nothing else. So `prettier --check .` flagged it
# the moment the harness reformatted it mid-round, and `npm run verify` went
# red on a file this repository does not track, cannot format and does not own.
# That is the same shape as the generated-artefact block above: a gate whose
# outcome depends on what a tool did since the last clean. Anchored, and to the
# one file rather than to `.claude/`, whose `skills/` subdirectory is source.
/.claude/settings.local.json

# The subagent-driven-development workspace: one directory per plan, holding
# that plan's ledger, task briefs, implementer reports, reviews and review
# packages. Scratch for running the work, never part of the delivered
# repository.
#
# IT WAS NEVER ACTUALLY IGNORED, and `.prettierignore` has called it
# "Git-ignored scratch" since Phase 0 — a comment asserting something untrue of
# the repository it sits in, which is the exact defect species this project has
# spent three phases catching in its own prose. Nothing was committed by
# accident only because §8.1 forbids `git add -A` without reading the diff;
# that is a convention standing in for a guarantee. Found by the Task 8
# implementer while diagnosing an unrelated gate failure, not by a review.
#
# Anchored to the root: a `packages/*/\.superpowers` would be a different thing
# and should not be swept up by this line.
/.superpowers/

# Editor and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# The handoff archive — the extracted folder is tracked instead
*.zip

# Visual baselines are generated in the pinned Playwright container and
# committed as `-linux.png` only. A run on a developer's host asks for a
# `-win32`/`-darwin` name, finds none, and WRITES one — after which every
# later run compares the host against itself and passes while the committed
# baselines go unread. 31 such files were found untracked here. e2e/visual
# .spec.ts skips off Linux; this refuses the files if it is ever bypassed.
e2e/**/*-win32.png
e2e/**/*-darwin.png
